Assistant A is the better response. Its page is centered, disables scrolling, and uses a full-screen video with the requested heading and hover effect. It does not make the button faded in its initial state, though: the transparent background alone is not the same as reduced opacity. Its video source is also a placeholder, but it clearly tells the user to replace it.

Assistant B has more substantial issues. Its video has no source and is styled as a small, rounded, in-flow element rather than a full-screen background; the flex layout would place it beside the hero content. It also does not reliably prevent scrolling, and the button starts at full opacity—the hover rule sets opacity to `1` as well, so there is no fade transition. The extra “Join us now!” text was not requested.

Neither response fully implements the faded initial button state, but A is much closer to the requested design. My example uses reduced initial opacity and a fixed, viewport-filling video to address those details.

My final verdict is: [[A>>B]]

