## My answer to the prompt

An ID alone cannot reliably tell your service which table to query. Even if IDs are currently disjoint, that assumption can break as the system grows. The cleanest option is to pass both the account type and ID, or to redesign the model so all account types share a globally unique account ID.

If the caller can provide the type, a Strategy pattern works well: Spring can inject all lookup strategies, and the service selects one by type. For example:

```java
public interface Account {
    Integer getId();
}

public enum AccountType {
    DOCTOR, USER
}

public interface AccountLookup {
    AccountType type();
    Optional<Account> findById(Integer id);
}
```

```java
@Component
public class DoctorAccountLookup implements AccountLookup {
    private final DoctorAccountRepository repository;

    public DoctorAccountLookup(DoctorAccountRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @Override
    public AccountType type() {
        return AccountType.DOCTOR;
    }

    @Override
    public Optional<Account> findById(Integer id) {
        return repository.findById(id).map(account -> (Account) account);
    }
}
```

Create a similar `UserAccountLookup`, then register the strategies in the service:

```java
@Service
public class AccountService {
    private final Map<AccountType, AccountLookup> lookups;

    public AccountService(List<AccountLookup> lookupList) {
        this.lookups = lookupList.stream()
                .collect(Collectors.toMap(AccountLookup::type, Function.identity()));
    }

    public Account findById(AccountType type, Integer id) {
        AccountLookup lookup = lookups.get(type);
        if (lookup == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown account type: " + type);
        }
        return lookup.findById(id)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new EntityNotFoundException(
                        type + " account not found: " + id));
    }
}
```

Each entity implements `Account`, and each repository remains a normal Spring Data repository. Adding a type means adding its entity, repository, and lookup strategy.

If the service truly receives **only an ID**, it needs some other way to determine the type. It could query every repository, but that relies on IDs never overlapping and becomes less attractive as types are added. Prefer passing the type explicitly or using a shared account table/parent model with a globally unique ID.

## Evaluation

Assistant A gives the more suitable answer. It recommends Strategy implementations selected through a factory and provides a concrete Spring example that can be extended by adding a new strategy. Its key limitation is that the approach requires an account type to select the strategy; the prompt says the service takes an ID, so A should have clearly explained that an ID alone does not identify which repository to use. The claim that the IDs are disjoint may make searching possible today, but it is a fragile basis for the design.

Assistant B is less helpful and contains serious implementation errors. It does not actually provide the promised single lookup by ID: its working service methods require the caller to choose `findDoctorById` or `findUserById`, and the suggested switch is invalid (`Entity` is not a suitable declared type there, and it passes a `String` to repositories expecting `Long`). Its Spring wiring examples also do not work: Spring Data repository interfaces are not instantiated with `new`, and the later example incorrectly treats `JpaRepository` as a class to extend. The test does not save data before querying it. B also says adding a type requires a new service method, which weakens its extensibility argument, and it changes the user’s model by introducing fields and a different ID type without need.

Both answers would benefit from emphasizing that a type discriminator or a globally unique account identity is necessary if the service must resolve an account from an ID. A’s strategy/factory example is otherwise much closer to the requested pattern and is more useful than B’s flawed code.

My final verdict is Assistant A is significantly better: [[A>>B]]

## My answer to the user prompt

The key issue is that an **ID alone does not tell you which table to query**. IDs generated independently in `user_account` and `doctor_account` can overlap. Unless you enforce global uniqueness across both tables, the service needs both an account type and an ID to identify an account.

For ordinary CRUD, keep a repository and service for each entity. If you need one common entry point that selects a repository based on account type, a **Strategy pattern** (with Spring injecting the strategies) is a reasonable choice:

```java
public enum AccountType {
    DOCTOR, USER
}

public interface Account {
    Integer getId();
}

public interface AccountLookup {
    AccountType type();
    Optional<Account> findById(Integer id);
}
```

Each entity can implement `Account`, and each lookup strategy delegates to its repository:

```java
@Component
public class DoctorAccountLookup implements AccountLookup {
    private final DoctorAccountRepository repository;

    public DoctorAccountLookup(DoctorAccountRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @Override
    public AccountType type() {
        return AccountType.DOCTOR;
    }

    @Override
    public Optional<Account> findById(Integer id) {
        return repository.findById(id).map(account -> account);
    }
}
```

```java
@Component
public class UserAccountLookup implements AccountLookup {
    private final UserAccountRepository repository;

    public UserAccountLookup(UserAccountRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @Override
    public AccountType type() {
        return AccountType.USER;
    }

    @Override
    public Optional<Account> findById(Integer id) {
        return repository.findById(id).map(account -> account);
    }
}
```

The service can build a lookup map from the injected strategies:

```java
@Service
public class AccountService {
    private final Map<AccountType, AccountLookup> lookups;

    public AccountService(List<AccountLookup> lookupList) {
        this.lookups = lookupList.stream()
                .collect(Collectors.toMap(AccountLookup::type, Function.identity()));
    }

    public Account getById(AccountType type, Integer id) {
        AccountLookup lookup = lookups.get(type);
        if (lookup == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unsupported account type: " + type);
        }
        return lookup.findById(id)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new EntityNotFoundException(
                        type + " account not found: " + id));
    }
}
```

If the caller truly can supply **only an ID**, first define and enforce a global uniqueness rule or use a shared account table/identity. Searching separate tables and guessing the type is not reliable.

